{ [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Ah the english. My favourite. Now if you want to get your opponent out of the comfort zone, play the english. (A warning: It is a little complex, so if you are new to the english and do not understand it, no matter. Just move on to the next chapter) To play the English, Play c4 }
1. c4!! { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] This move stops the move d5 and is better for you queen and knight } 1... e5 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Black plays e5, The Reversed Sicillian. Follow the arrows to learn this magnificent opening! } { [%csl Gc3][%cal Gb1c3] } 2. Nc3 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] This move discourages d5 and gets the knight out. } 2... Nf6 { [%csl Gf3][%cal Gg1f3] } 3. Nf3 Nc6 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] This move by black discourages us to play d4. But now play g3 to fianchetto the bishop. } { [%csl Gg3][%cal Gg2g3] } 4. g3 d5 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Here black plays d5, Trying to attack the pawn. Play cxd5! } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gc4d5] } 5. cxd5 Nxd5 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Fianchetto } { [%csl Gg2][%cal Gf1g2] } 6. Bg2 Nb6 { [%csl Gg1][%cal Ge1g1] } 7. O-O { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Now you have played the english opening and made you king safer by castling. This opening is one of my favourites and i hope after this chapter and a few more games after this study, yours too. } *


