{ [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] I realized that i put a lot of white's openings. It is time that i show you that black ain't lesser than white. The Caro-Kann Defense is a reliable option that immediately challenges white to play carefully. }
1. e4 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Play c6 } { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 1... c6!! { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] This is the Caro-Kann Defense. It is quite underrated in my opinion. White normally plays d4. } 2. d4 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Play d5 to question white's e4 pawn. } { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 2... d5! { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] This is the setup of the Caro-Kann Defense. As you can see here, the d pawn is questioning the e-pawn of white. there are a number of variations in the Caro. I will show you the most common of them in the next chapter } *


