{ [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] The most common variation of the Caro-Kann. The advanced. Do you remember the first moves? }
1. e4 c6 2. d4 { And Now? } 2... d5 3. e5 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Here white plays e5, not taking the pawn. You have to play Bf5. } { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 3... Bf5 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] The bishop is very well placed here at f5. Here white plays Nf3. } 4. Nf3 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Nf3 is played to support the e and d pawns of white and develops the knight to a good square. Now play e5 to block the e pawn from advancing forward. } { [%csl Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 4... e6 5. Be2 { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] White plays Be2 to block the upcoming pin from black from Bg4 } { [%csl Gd7][%cal Gb8d7] } 5... Nd7 6. O-O { [%anno "ArjunShukla23", arjunshukla23] Alrighty, this is the position of the Caro-Kann Advance Variation. } *


